#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Why each failed rollout failed, which skills each model lacks, and which failures the reward can teach.

Reads any verifiers traces.jsonl (docs/FAILURES.md has the taxonomy, every rule
and its blind spots). Layers, in order:

  1 root cause   deterministic, every failed rollout: one category from the
                 grade status, the diagnostics, the stop reason or the reply,
                 with the evidence that produced it (the matched line, the
                 pin, the broken rule)
  2 event logs   only for miss, self_check_failed and never_finishes: the
                 submission and the task's formal gold rerun in the task image
                 through an Executor, with a monitor bound to uart_rx and
                 uart_tx; both event logs and their first divergence. The
                 grader's verdict stays the one in the trace.
  3 residual     opt-in (--label-residual), only for the layer-2 set: one label
                 from a model that is not evaluated, recorded under
                 runs/failures/<run-id>/labels/; --replay rebuilds every label
                 with no model call. Never affects a reward.
  4 features     pass@1 per task joined with the proposer's witness depth,
                 mechanisms and module
  5 RL views     pass@k, learnability per category, fix channel, success by
                 output-token quartile
  6 validation   results/failures/handcheck.csv (blind) and --agreement

Rollouts with no grade, and Verilator internal errors, are infrastructure:
excluded from every rate and listed separately.

Usage:
  tools/failure_analysis TRACES.jsonl ... --propose-run RUN [--written written.json]
                         [--executor prime --concurrency N] [--label-residual] [--tag T]
  tools/failure_analysis --resume runs/failures/<run-id> [--label-residual]  # finish a run
  tools/failure_analysis --replay runs/failures/<run-id>   # no model calls, no simulations
  tools/failure_analysis --agreement results/failures/handcheck.csv
  tools/failure_analysis --simulate-batch IN.json --out OUT.json [-j N]   # in the task image
Outputs: results/failures/{labels.jsonl,summary.md,handcheck.csv,exemplars/}.
Same inputs and recorded run give byte-identical outputs.
"""

